WEBVTT

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:29.740
to the people.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:39.820
prehistoric Britain set up this great stone circle on Salisbury Plain. What is

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:52.070
the meaning of Stonehenge? Were Britain's other rings of stone centres of an

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:59.580
unknown pagan cult? Were they places of sacrifice and death?

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.920
Were they observatories where 4,000 years ago

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.440
astronomers plotted the courses of the sun, moon and stars?

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.180
Down is the door.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.180
Mysteries from the Files of Arthur C. Clarke,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.740
author of 2001 and inventor of the communications satellite.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.720
Now in retreat in Sri Lanka,

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.580
after a lifetime of science, space and writing,

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.760
he ponders the riddles of this and other worlds.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.200
Everyone must feel awe, indeed reverence,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.170
at a place like Stonehenge.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.880
But these latter-day Druids

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.500
have no more right to be there than anyone else.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:01:59.980
Their association with Stonehenge...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.800
is an invention of 18th century Romantic writers.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:11.610
The Druids flourished 1,000 years after the completion of Stonehenge,

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.500
so to confuse them with stone circles

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:20.510
is like mixing up the Battle of Britain and the Battle of Hastings.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.840
This is one of the few facts we do know about the 1,000 or so stone circles

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.770
scattered over the British Isles.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.970
I'm standing amongst the 2,000-year-old...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.990
ruins of Anuradhapura, the ancient capital of Sri Lanka.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.890
The Romans sent their ambassadors here

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.130
before the birth of Christ.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.340
We know the exact history of this city

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.780
because its builders left written records.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.300
Indeed, we know what it looked like in its glory

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.030
because its greatest shrines are now being restored.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:41.610
By contrast, the hundreds of stone circles scattered over the British Isles are a complete mystery.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.520
Their builders left no record of their thoughts or their motives.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:53.710
More than a thousand circles and henges have survived into modern times.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.970
The power of their lingering...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.260
magic has outlasted the rise

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.780
and sometimes the ruin

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:26.220
of Christianity. These are

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.380
empty places scattered over the British

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.860
Isles. The ground

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:40.930
stand on holds few clues to their purpose but in some circles the layout of the stones themselves

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:47.950
may indicate the answer at long meg in cumbria the sun sets behind this megalith only on midwinter's

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:55.180
day so did standing stones help to fix the calendar in prehistoric time nowhere on earth

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.800
is there more spectacular proof of ancient fascination with the changing skies than here.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.720
This is Newgrange Tomb in Ireland.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.250
Set on a hilltop above the River Boyne,

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.310
it is an almost unknown wonder.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.550
Yet this is the oldest building in the world.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.660
Faced with gleaming and immaculate quartz,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.080
it was built five centuries before the pyramids.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.360
It is 5,000 years old.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:48.150
Every year, on one special day, Newgrange tomb is a place of pilgrimage for the archaeologist who restored it, Professor Michael O'Kelly.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:56.460
He comes here in the pre-dawn darkness because this is December the 21st, the winter solstice, the shortest day of the year.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:21.720
Professor O'Kelly began to unlock the secret of Newgrange when he uncovered a strange slit above the door.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.920
At first, its purpose baffled him.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:33.280
The tomb has an inner chamber magnificently vaulted.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.620
Oddly, it contained the bones of just four adults and one child.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:45.510
His hunch was that the slit above the door was to let the sun into the tomb

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.480
to wake the spirits of the dead.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.440
This would probably happen, he thought, at dawn on midwinter's day.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:10.490
His hunch proved to be correct.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.730
Through the slit on the shortest day of the year

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.470
come the first rays of the sun

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:27.990
as it rises above the ridge on the far side of the river Boyne.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:34.910
Michael O'Kelly will never forget the first time he witnessed the astounding phenomenon

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:39.410
which the builders of Newgrange designed their Stone Age cathedral to capture.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:48.970
The first shot of orange-red light penetrated right to where I was sitting,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.460
gradually widened to a 17-centimetre band,

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.380
illuminated the whole interior.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.780
I could feel the spirits of the dead all round about me.

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:14.550
And I wondered if a voice shouldn't come and say something,

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.650
even if only to tell me to get the hell out of here.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:20.920
But nothing happened. Silence.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.380
The band of light slowly crossed the floor, and then a point came.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:34.180
After 17 minutes, sudden cutoff, darkness.

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:40.830
How to explain it? I don't know, but quite obviously,

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:44.940
the builders of this place had thought the whole thing out,

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:51.880
had organized this slit so that it would be level with the local horizon,

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.200
and so that when the tip of the sun appears,

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.960
there's a horizontal line from the horizon.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.840
and through the slit right to the centre of the floor.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:09.850
The world's oldest building guards one of the world's oldest mysteries.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.200
Perhaps people gathered here 5,000 years ago

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.120
to commune with the spirits of the dead.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.360
Perhaps this unique dawn marked the end of the old year,

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:20.680
the beginning of the new.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.040
Whatever the truth, the marvel of New Grange

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:27.080
displays the central importance of the movements of the heavens

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.180
to the builders of the ancient world.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.340
Far to the north, in the Scottish Highlands, Dr. Archie Tom believes he has proof

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:46.620
that standing stones mark full-scale observatories used by prehistoric astronomers.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.900
His theory, first suggested by his father, an Oxford professor,

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.940
is that the stones were aligned with distinctive features on the horizon.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.980
In this case, a cleft in the hills, the paps of Jura.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:10.880
26 miles away. The TOMS surveys suggested that the astronomers stood on this platform known to

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.740
be man-made. In an age when Scotland enjoyed fine weather, they could fix the date of the winter

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:22.120
solstice by standing in line with the stone and watching the sun set precisely in the cleft of the

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:22.840
hills of Jura.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.360
The Toms believe ancient astronomers used observatories like this

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:44.450
to fix their calendars for the business and ceremonial of their year.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:55.230
A computer has already established what seem to be dozens of astronomical alignments at Stonehenge.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:10:59.970
But strange pits on the...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.820
top of the Stonehenge lintels have inspired this American schoolmaster

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:11.420
Richard Brinkerhoff to suggest an even more exotic astronomical theory. He

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:21.840
believes the ancient astronomers used the top of Stonehenge as a catwalk. From

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.000
there, with the help of sighting poles stuck into the pits, they could observe

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.340
important developments in the cycles of the moon.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:37.240
There are 11 pits altogether, probably, perhaps more obscured by later erosion.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:44.940
These two particular holes, like all the others, were probably used as sites

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:56.620
by people on top of the lintels yonder, looking past them at moonrise on the distant horizon.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:59.970
The northernmost point that the moon ever rises on the horizon...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:06.990
as seen from across the circle is identified precisely

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.840
by the farthest of these pits behind me,

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.840
above which the moon rises once every 57 years.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.720
The northernmost point that the sun rises

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:27.130
is identified by, seen by a man across the circle,

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.960
is identified by the moon.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:44.060
The pit right there in front of all of the pits between these two identify intermediate stages in the moon's motion northward along the horizon in those regions where the sunrise never occurred.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:59.960
There's no doubt that some of these stone circles do have astronomical alignment.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.970
But this doesn't mean that their builders were skilled astronomers.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:12.420
As for the other circles, the majority, well, perhaps there were no more than meeting places,

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.630
the equivalent of the village hall, if you like.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:22.060
We can't prove this because all these structures disappeared except for the basic stones.

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:28.530
Look at this forest of columns around me. No one would ever have guessed what this was.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:29.610
In fact...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.820
These pillars were the foundations of a very tall building, many stories high, built about 2,000 years ago.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:41.640
But all the wood has vanished, so today it's impossible to guess what the original structure was like.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:45.970
And that may be the case with the still unexplained stone circles.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:53.350
But the incredible scale of this circle at Avebury in Wiltshire almost defies modern explanation.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:57.390
It covers 28 and a half acres and encompasses a whole village.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:13:59.980
In the ancient world...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.360
Some experts believe it must have been such a showpiece that Stonehenge with its weird arches was built later as a rival attraction

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:24.380
Avebury must have been a massive undertaking. Its construction leaves archaeologists awestruck. Dr. Aubrey Burr

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:29.960
The amazing thing is that places like Avebury and Stonehenge were built with tools as simple as

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.720
There's this antler pick which I've just driven into the ground with a lump of stone.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:42.510
And the people who built these places would use tools like this to break up the chalk which lies underneath all this grass and earth,

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:51.420
lever it out into huge blocks, and then, having done that, they'd take another very, very simple bit of equipment they had,

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:58.240
the shoulder blade of an ox, they'd use this to shovel up the chalk into wickerwork baskets.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.550
which of course was as hard as rock it's not as soft as the sort of stuff we use in in classrooms

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:10.580
it really is very very hard they get it into a basket like this and then hefting it up onto

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:17.560
their shoulder they'd carry this up the sides of the ditch to the bank where they'd dump load

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:26.160
after load after load of it until the bank was 30 or 40 feet above them now the amazing thing is

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:29.600
that this ditch where I'm standing

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:38.190
is only the top because when it was excavated just before the first world war it was discovered that

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:45.790
it's at least 18 to 20 feet deeper than this all this is just material that has fallen in over the

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:52.340
last 4 000 years and when the ditch was first dug it would have been sheer sided it was so steep in

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:57.950
fact that the workers had to leave little steps in the chalk so they could lift the chalk up and

00:15:57.950 --> 00:15:59.930
out up to the ditch and it would have

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.910
been so deep that had someone had a telegraph pole at the bottom rising up here at the top would

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:12.640
not have shown above the sides of the ditch. It's an incredible undertaking and it must have taken

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:19.710
50, 60 generations of people. People who would be born in the middle of the work, would work all

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:27.740
their lives at it and yet would never see it finished. And then inside the ditch were the great

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:28.920
circles of stones.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.060
they were dragged several miles from the Marlborough Downs to Avebury. The stones were

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:41.850
carefully selected for size and shape. When we look at a huge thing like this, it wasn't moved

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:46.830
very easily, but what isn't obvious is the work that went on before they moved the stone, because

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:51.820
they couldn't just get hold of something like this and drag it. First of all, they had to make

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:56.500
sledges, and they had to make sledges of oak or elm, something very, very tough and resilient.

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:59.980
They would also need ropes. Now, they hadn't...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.160
got the sort of rope or horse we have today they'd have to kill hundreds and hundreds of cattle

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:13.360
probably to flay their heights and wind them bind them into very thick skeins of leather so there

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:19.610
then we have a stone weighing 20 30 perhaps as much as 60 tons on its sledge and there are people

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:28.400
dragging on these great leather ropes but for every ton a stone weighs you need perhaps four people

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:29.980
to drag on them.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:42.100
And so for a stone like this, this is this 50, 60-ton stone, you need at least 200 people dragging and hauling, and the stone is moving almost imperceptibly.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:49.580
Some of these stones have been re-erected in modern times. In 1934, for example, quite a small stone was put upright.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:55.490
It only weighed 8 tons, and yet it took 12 men five days using steel horses.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:07.580
At circles like Castle Rig in Cumbria, the construction was easier, but the layout is often puzzling.

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:11.120
Many seem to have been deliberately designed to be non-circular.

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:13.600
Penman Moor in Wales is an ellipse.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:18.550
Borough Hill in Roxbrusher is one of many shaped like an egg.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:25.080
Castle Rig itself is a flattened circle.

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:28.980
Such precise patterns are repeated all over the country.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.320
16.25. 16.25 metres.

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:36.690
Studying this strange geometry is the hobby of Dr John Edwin Wood,

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:38.980
for it's not a case of bad drawing.

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:41.810
Oddly, the early rings are perfect circles.

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:46.090
Deducing how Castle Rig was laid out is a challenge.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:49.240
We think that this was laid out quite simply,

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:52.650
starting with a number of equilateral triangles.

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:58.230
An equilateral triangle is very easy to produce on the ground.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:18:59.630
You start off with an open field.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:02.310
and you cut strings all of the same length,

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.720
and then you pull them taut at the corners,

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.500
and that gives you an equilateral triangle.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.710
And this was built up with four equilateral triangles

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.520
with ropes and pegs like this.

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:19.290
One, two, three, four.

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:25.800
And I put a peg there, and a peg there, peg here, and a peg here.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:29.900
Well, we think that it was probably laid out like this.

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:37.140
Now, this represents Castle Rig, and the stones are shown by the black blobs on the plan.

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:43.980
And these pins are the positions where they would have had to put the pegs in to lay out the stone circle.

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:55.120
And we think they started with a rope from that pin, direct to this one, and then round to make the circumference of the circle start there.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:59.800
And notice how it swings round to that one, and then it comes free to give you an arc of a...

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.570
of a circle of a different radius, and when you get to here,

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:07.270
it catches up again on this one to make the other little arc.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:09.870
And now the next thing that they would have done

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:17.900
is to transfer the rope to that peg and complete the rest of it

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:20.480
by allowing it to swing round the centre peg all the way

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:28.010
so that finally you'll get the full geometry of the castle rig ring

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.660
without anything more elaborate.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:31.080
and strings and pegs.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.730
The stone circles of the British Isles

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:41.050
are strange, indeed haunting places.

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:44.380
Many of them have precise astronomical alignments,

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:46.330
which must have required great skill.

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.330
For others, the geometry is very obscure.

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:52.610
We simply don't understand the motives of the designers.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:56.570
But there are clues from this circle in Aberdeenshire

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:59.710
that these places were indeed the cathedrals or temples.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.000
of the ancient world.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.940
Well, most of their local stones are granite.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:08.540
Castle Fraser has been studied by Dr. Aubrey Burl.

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:14.940
There's an extraordinary feature in the Aberdeenshire circles, a great stone lying on its side, flanked

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:15.880
by two uprights.

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:21.630
Well, this stone's a recumbent, and you can see why it's called that, because it's just

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.490
lying flat between these two huge stones.

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:27.730
One of the remarkable things about these stones is that they're absolutely horizontal.

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:29.980
Um, if we put on a spirit level...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.210
on it just like that, look where the bubble comes, right in the centre. The people obviously

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:37.790
have shifted and balanced these stones because they have bases that are shaped like this

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:42.600
and they can be rocked to and fro until they're absolutely level, then they bang these huge

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:47.680
chalk stones in underneath. The presence of the recumbent stone has given Dr. Burl

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:53.960
an insight into the minds of the circle builders. These are places of dread. People who built

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:59.970
these circles were people who had very insecure lives. They had no knowledge of what...

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:05.360
caused a blizzard or a drought or an epidemic, they saw their children dying, they saw their

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:10.450
crops failing. Not every time, but when it did happen, they had no means of stopping

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:13.990
it because they had no understanding of it. Now, if you're in that sort of situation,

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:20.440
you either take an attitude of que sera, sera, what will be, will be, or else you try to

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:26.160
do something about it. In other words, you're either fatalistic or else you try to be adventuresome

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:29.740
and try to intercede with whatever is causing these disasters.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:39.550
The recumbent stone told Dr. Burl what the people who built this circle turned to for help in their uncertain world.

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:46.000
He found the stone had been placed between the southeastern and southwestern points.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:52.510
They were concerned with one and one phenomenon only.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.740
That was the moon up in the sky.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:59.110
Because the moon rises towards the southeast.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:07.420
it sets towards the southwest and there are nearly 50 recumbent stone circles where it's possible to

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:13.030
work out exactly in which direction a recumbent stone is directed and every one of them is directed

00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:18.930
just to that position where the moon would pass between one flanker and the other flanker

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:24.190
passing over the recumbent stone and then setting the moon must have been an object

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:29.870
i think of very special reverence if that's the right word to these people

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:32.260
We don't know how they regarded the moon,

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:36.520
but we do know, even from today's astronomical observations,

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:41.060
that to pass from one flanker to the next,

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:44.450
the moon must have been slowly moving across the recumbent

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:46.440
for one, perhaps two hours.

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:48.480
And it must have been at that time

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:50.640
that the people performed their ceremonies.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:52.540
But there's rather more than that.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.160
The moon has a very peculiar motion in the sky,

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:59.980
and once about every 19 years, the moon is very, very low.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:06.250
on the southern horizon very low indeed and at that time instead of being high up it would have

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:11.050
appeared almost to roll along the top of the recumbent stone a magical moment for the people

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:16.970
although there are some clues to ceremonies enacted on nights of the full moon the ashes of

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:22.980
fires even the pitiful remains of many different children the stones keep their eerie secrets when

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:29.070
we look at circles like these at least i am was very much aware of a mystery surrounding them

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:29.630
because...

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:33.780
We can collect so many facts about them,

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:36.040
we can make so many deductions about them,

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.080
and yet in the end, when we stand in a ring like this

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.640
on a beautiful, moonlit night,

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:46.450
I am more aware of mysteries than of answers.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:50.300
I can see shadows, but what I can't see,

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.220
however hard I try, are the people who built them.

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:55.890
I know something about their lives,

00:24:57.100 --> 00:24:59.980
I know their lives were shorter, I know their children...

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.180
I can understand why they put up these stones,

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:07.960
but what actually went on inside the rings,

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:10.590
the actual beliefs of the people.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.940
These are matters which escape me,

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.560
and I suspect will always escape us.

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.910
You know, it's like reaching out into the darkness.

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:24.730
You can go so far, but in the end you can never touch them.

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.350
You're always reaching for a shadow.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:30.180
Thank you.

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:04.490
Next week, the things that can fall from the sky.
